  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 AM CDT Fri Jun 26 2026\n\nToday and Tonight\n\nAn embedded shortwave trough in the zonal flow will move across\nnorthern Minnesota and northern Wisconsin today and upper \nMichigan tonight and another shortwave trough will move across \nIowa this morning and northern Illinois and southern Great Lakes\nthis afternoon and tonight. Surface-baased CAPES will be \ngenerally less than 200 J/kg. The best surface to 850 mb \nmoisture convergence will be to our north and south. As a \nresult, it is not surprising that the CAMs are mainly dry across\nthe Upper Mississippi River Valley through tonight.\n\nHigh temperatures will be in the mid- and upper 70s this afternoon \nand the low temperatures will be in the 50s tonight.\n\nSaturday\n\nA 500 mb ridge will slowly build across the area on Saturday. This \nwill keep the area dry. High temperatures will be around 80 degrees.\n\nSaturday night and Sunday\n\nA warm front will produce scattered showers and storms as it moves \nnortheast through the area on Saturday night and Sunday. With most-\nunstable CAPES less than 200 J/kg and weak shear 0-3 km and 0-6 km \nshear, not anticipating any organized severe weather. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s. High \ntemperatures on Sunday will range from the upper 70s to upper 80s.\n\nSunday night through Monday\n\nAs the western US trough digs, the upper level ridge will build \nnorthward toward Hudson Bay. Southerly winds will increase 850 mb \ntemperatures into the 24 to 26C on Monday. These temperatures are 2 \nto 3 standard deviations above normal. This will result in high \ntemperatures in the lower to mid-90s on Monday. With dew points in \nthe 70s, heat indices would range from the 100 to 110. This could \nresult in an Extreme Heat Warning.\n\nMonday night and Thursday\n\nThe models continue to show that as a series of shortwaves eject out \nof the western longwave trough from Monday night through Thursday, \nthe upper level ridge will gradually flatten. These shortwaves will \nbe close enough that they will provide a chance for periodic showers \nand storms. With strong and and very strong instability across the \nregion, some of these storms may become severe. This will be highly \ndependent upon the timing and the 0-3 km and 0-6 km shear.\n\nHigh temperatures will range from the upper 80s to mid-90s. Heat \nindices will likely range from 100 to 110 on Tuesday and range from \n95 to 105 Wednesday and Thursday. The confidence on the high \ntemperatures and heat indices are lower due to the potential of \nshowers and storm. If Wednesday remains similar to Monday and \nTuesday, an Extreme Heat Warning may be needed even those the heat \nindices are lower. This is due to the longevity of this extreme heat.",
